<div class="header"><h2>msg in us military rations</h2></div>
<div class="body">

<p>The U.S. military was one of the earliest institutional adopters of MSG and remains the one major American food supply chain that <strong>does not follow civilian FDA labeling</strong> for the ingredient. Field rations are procured through the Defense Logistics Agency under a closed logistics system, so the "monosodium glutamate" labeling rule (see <a href="#section-100-what-is-msg-dsg">what is msg/dsg</a>) does not apply. This section covers the timeline and the modern MRE recipe, and flags one implication for the consumer side of the rest of the site.</p>

<h3>post-wwii adoption: korea and vietnam</h3>
<p>Post-WWII research into flavor enhancers for bland, shelf-stable rations found that adding MSG measurably improved soldier acceptance. Ac'cent, a domestic MSG brand, was launched in the late 1940s and became a topic at military food symposiums during the Korean War. The broader MSG timeline up to the 1950s is told in <a href="#section-150-history">history</a>; this section focuses on the post-adoption evolution <sup><a href="#src-military">military</a>, <a href="#src-military-2">military-2</a></sup>.</p>

<h3>the mre recipe: msg + dsg + hidden carriers</h3>
<p>Modern Meals, Ready-to-Eat (MREs) use the same synergistic umami trick the civilian food industry uses (see <a href="#section-300-the-problem">the problem</a> for the synergy claim and <a href="#section-450-solutions">solutions</a> for what civilians can do about it), in roughly three layers:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Added MSG and Disodium Guanylate (DSG, E627)</strong> &mdash; DSG is the disodium salt of guanylic acid, a ribonucleotide that boosts MSG's umami effect several-fold. Both can appear on the MRE ingredient deck when added, but in practice the military groups them under "flavorings" or "seasonings" in proprietary spice blends. Cation-swapped variants of the same chemistry are covered in <a href="#section-105-msg-with-other-cations">msg with other cations</a> <sup><a href="#src-military-3">military-3</a>, <a href="#src-military-4">military-4</a></sup>.</li>
<li><strong>Hidden-glutamate carriers</strong> &mdash; autolyzed yeast, hydrolyzed vegetable protein, soy protein isolate, sodium caseinate. These are the same names the FDA lists as naturally glutamate-rich; the labeling gap is the same one analyzed in <a href="#section-140-yeast-extract-and-sodium-can-form-msg-during-cooking">yeast extract and sodium can form msg during cooking</a> for the civilian side, except that in MREs the label exemption is by design rather than by interpretation <sup><a href="#src-military-3">military-3</a></sup>.</li>
<li><strong>Sodium load</strong> &mdash; a standard MRE carries roughly 1,500&ndash;2,500 mg of sodium per meal, of which MSG and DSG are only a small fraction. MSG contains about 60% less sodium by weight than table salt, so the bulk of the sodium comes from added salt and from the sodium-bearing ingredients themselves <sup><a href="#src-military-2">military-2</a></sup>.</li>
</ul>

<h3>why this matters for the consumer side of this site</h3>
<p>MREs are the cleanest natural experiment in long-term, high-dose exposure to a MSG + DSG + hidden-glutamate combination available &mdash; soldiers eat them three meals a day, often for years at a stretch, with no consumer-style labeling and no opt-out. If the chronic high-dose combination were producing the dramatic effects the worst-case critics of synergy worry about, the military medical literature would be the place to look. This site does not take a position on what that literature does or does not show; the consumer-side risks that <em>are</em> documented in the literature are listed in <a href="#section-200-health-concerns">health concerns</a>. The point of this section is only that the military food system is, structurally, the one U.S. environment where the labeling rules the rest of the site complains about do not apply <sup><a href="#src-military-2">military-2</a></sup>.</p>

</div>
